          %p whence these three subjects are but three perspectives on a single underlying phenomenon.
          %p
            This insight dates from the late 1970s; an early record is
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#LambekScott86"} Lambek &amp; Scott 86
            %em trilogy
            (
            %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trilogy"}> Wikipedia
            \: “three works of art that are connected and can be seen either as a single work or as three individual works”) in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#Mellies06"} Melliès 06, Sec. 1
          %div{:style => "margin: -40px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/MelliesClassicalComputationalTrilogy.jpg", :width => "510px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                From
                %a{:href => "#Mellies06"} Melliès 06
          %p
            (Notice that
            %a{:href => "#Mellies06"} Melliès 06
            on
            %a{:href => "https://hal.archives-ouvertes.fr/hal-00154243/document#page=3"} p.2
            does mean to regard
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%CE%BB-calculus"} λ-calculus
            as
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/programming+language"} programming language
          %p
            In
            %a{:href => "#Harper11"} Harper 11
            the profoundness of the trilogy inspires the following emphatic prose, alluding to the doctrinal position of ‘trinitarianism’:
          %blockquote
            %p The central dogma of computational trinitarianism holds that Logic, Languages, and Categories are but three manifestations of one divine notion of computation. There is no preferred route to enlightenment: each aspect provides insights that comprise the experience of computation in our lives.
            %p Computational trinitarianism entails that any concept arising in one aspect should have meaning from the perspective of the other two. If you arrive at an insight that has importance for logic, languages, and categories, then you may feel sure that you have elucidated an essential concept of computation–you have made an enduring scientific discovery.
          %p
            For more detailed review see
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Eades12"} Eades 12, Sec. 3
          %h4#ClassicalParametrized Parametrized
          %p More is true: Since
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                computation happens in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contexts"} contexts
                and is
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof+relevance"} proof relevant
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
                give rise to their
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codomain+fibration"} systems of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+categories"} slice categories
                and are in general
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
                may
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} depend
                on other types and are in general
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy types
          %p
            the traditional computational trilogy
            %a{:href => "#ClassicalPlain"} above
            enhances to read as follows:
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th
                  In
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent
                  %br/
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
                %th
                  In
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/programming+languages"} programming languages
                  %br/
                  and
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computation"} computation
                %th
                  In
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} locally cartesian closed
                  %br/
                  = succeed "/" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} (∞,1)-toposes
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  A
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term"} term
                  of some
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
                  %br/
                  in
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  A
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/program"} program
                  outputting some
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/data+type"} data type
                  %br/
                  in
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  A
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+element"} generalized element
                  of an
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
                  %br/
                  in a
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} slice
          %p
            See also
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Shulman18"} Shulman 18
          %p
            In this deeper form yet another equivalence – to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Proper+Orbifold+Cohomology"}> Sati Schreiber 20
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/schreiber/files/orbi210313.pdf#page=5"} p. 5
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+elements"} generalized elements
            in an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            may equivalently be regarded as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycles"} cocycles
            in (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-abelian+cohomology"}> non-abelian
            )
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+cohomology"} twisted cohomology
            if in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} slice (∞,1)-category
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Proper+Orbifold+Cohomology"} Sati Schreiber 20
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2008.01101.pdf#page=6"} p. 6
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/The+Character+Map+in+Twisted+Non-Abelian+Cohomology"} FSS 20
            = succeed ":" do
              %em computational tetralogy
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th
                  In
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent
                  %br/
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
                %th
                  In
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/programming+languages"} programming languages
                  %br/
                  and
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computation"} computation
                %th
                  In
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} locally cartesian closed
                  %br/
                  = succeed "/" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} ∞-categories
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} ∞-toposes
                %th
                  In
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-abelian+cohomology"} non-abelian
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
                  %br/
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parametrized+homotopy+theory"} param. homotopy theory
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  A
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term"} term
                  of some
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
                  %br/
                  in
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  A
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/program"} program
                  of some
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/data+type"} data type
                  %br/
                  in
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  An
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+element"} element
                  of an
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
                  %br/
                  in a
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} slice
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
